@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+# Primary-source prior art and novelty screen
+
+Checked 2026-10-26. Links below were opened or searched from their original
+projects and organizations. The family Samsung source is a mirror, not proof of
+matching installed source. Direct Samsung OSRC `uploadSearch?searchValue=M305F`
+returned HTTP 403 in this session; no exact `M305FDDS8CVB2` package has been
+downloaded. A negative web search is not proof no release exists.
+
+| Area | Already known | Independent M30 observation | Open question / falsifier | Potential contribution and weakness |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| SCSC HCI path | Family [`scsc_bluez.c`](https://github.com/akhil1999/android_kernel_samsung_m30lte/blob/095206579f69121278ed1dd5fd0d7975c0705c6a/drivers/misc/samsung/scsc_bt/scsc_bluez.c) registers `hci_dev`; [Kconfig](https://github.com/akhil1999/android_kernel_samsung_m30lte/blob/095206579f69121278ed1dd5fd0d7975c0705c6a/drivers/misc/samsung/scsc_bt/Kconfig) controls build. [BlueZ management API](https://github.com/bluez/bluez/blob/master/doc/mgmt-protocol.rst) presumes Linux HCI. | Running kernel has `CONFIG_BT=n`, bridge off; HAL owns `/dev/scsc_h4_0`. | Does exact stock source build with bridge and allow safe handoff? Failure to reproduce stock kernel falsifies a ready port. | Reproducible port/driver patch would be useful; a config fragment alone is too weak. Disabled bridge's post-free SKB read is source-only and not a proven installed vulnerability. |
+| SCSC firmware container | Family [`fwhdr.c`](https://github.com/akhil1999/android_kernel_samsung_m30lte/blob/095206579f69121278ed1dd5fd0d7975c0705c6a/drivers/misc/samsung/scsc/fwhdr.c), [`fwimage.c`](https://github.com/akhil1999/android_kernel_samsung_m30lte/blob/095206579f69121278ed1dd5fd0d7975c0705c6a/drivers/misc/samsung/scsc/fwimage.c) already specify header and CRCs. Linux [`ether_crc`](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/include/linux/crc32.h) is a reversed CRC32-LE value. | Local image has `smxf` 1.0/API 0.2, three matching CRCs and a build ID matching live firmware property. | Which sections map to R4/M4, which exact file is loaded, what enforces authenticity? Different boot log/path falsifies current load-path inference. | A robust multi-build parser and annotated boot map may make a technical article/tool; merely reimplementing documented offsets is not a conference result. |
+| SCSC Wi-Fi diagnostics | Family [`udi.c`](https://github.com/akhil1999/android_kernel_samsung_m30lte/blob/095206579f69121278ed1dd5fd0d7975c0705c6a/drivers/net/wireless/scsc/udi.c) logs FAPI with direction/timestamp and queues; [`cfg80211_ops.c`](https://github.com/akhil1999/android_kernel_samsung_m30lte/blob/095206579f69121278ed1dd5fd0d7975c0705c6a/drivers/net/wireless/scsc/cfg80211_ops.c) family modes omit monitor. | Live UDI node `/dev/s5n2560udi0` with `sec_wifi_device`; live `phy0`. | Can bounded logging recover own management/802.11 frames with useful metadata? If logs only Ethernet or omit channel/PHY, monitor-equivalence is false. | Decoder with completeness study and independent sniffer could be valuable. A flag patch or `mgmt_tx` presence is too weak. |
+| BLE advertising measurements | Android [advertiser API](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/bluetooth/le/BluetoothLeAdvertiser) defines callback semantics; [BlueZ advertisement](https://github.com/bluez/bluez/blob/master/doc/org.bluez.LEAdvertisement.rst) and [GATT example](https://github.com/bluez/bluez/blob/master/test/example-gatt-server) are prior fixture designs. [BlueZ `btmon`](https://github.com/bluez/bluez/blob/master/doc/btmon.rst) monitors HCI traffic. | Phone legacy advertiser succeeds; laptop sees LAB marker; GATT write/notify/reconnect now verified. | What is callback-to-air timing/loss under controlled conditions? If receiver observations track cached properties rather than scan events, packet-loss inference is invalid. | Reproducible measurement dataset/tool could make an article. One receiver trace is too weak for a general reliability claim. |
+| Target-specific disruption | [SIG HCI Disconnect](https://www.bluetooth.com/wp-content/uploads/Files/Specification/HTML/Core_v6.3/out/en/host-controller-interface/host-controller-interface-functional-specification.html) uses local connection handle. [SweynTooth original paper](https://www.usenix.org/conference/atc20/presentation/garbelini) tests named BLE implementations, typically with a connection. [BrakTooth research disclosure](https://asset-group.github.io/disclosures/braktooth/disclosure.html) lists named BR/EDR chipsets/versions and malformed LMP mechanisms. | No model/firmware evidence for the user's earbud link; no exploit or third-party disruption tested. | Does an exact owned target/version match an advisory, and can effect be independently measured? Non-match falsifies applicability. | New target-specific vulnerability with controls could justify disclosure; repeating an unrelated PoC or claiming universal deauth would not. |
+| Recent earbud advisories | CERT/CC [VU#472136](https://kb.cert.org/vuls/id/472136) (released 2026-01-15) names Redmi Buds 3 Pro–6 Pro; CVE-2025-13328 is RFCOMM command flooding/resource exhaustion and a firmware crash terminating paired links. It requires a named target/address and radio range, not a generic HCI command. CERT/CC [VU#859658](https://kb.cert.org/vuls/id/859658) (2026-09-08) names Skullcandy Dime 3 S2DCW firmware 1.0.0.28, CVE-2025-20701; unauthorized pairing can interrupt audio; vendor says 1.0.0.30 addresses it. | No target match; `com.noise.audio` has BES OTA candidate UUIDs but no confirmed earbud model. | Exact identity and firmware needed before relevance or bounded validation. | A new finding must show a new affected product/security issue; these advisories do not transfer to unknown earbuds. |
+| Nearby popup claims | Android advertisements are scanner data; [Android advertiser API](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/bluetooth/le/BluetoothLeAdvertiser) does not specify remote UI. Flipper stock [Bluetooth setting](https://docs.flipper.net/zero/basics/settings) describes BLE phone/remote use; [firmware application list](https://github.com/flipperdevices/flipperzero-firmware/blob/dev/applications/ReadMe.md) separates BLE HID from other radio apps. | LAB marker yields `PING` only on our opt-in Linux receiver. Noise firmware-lab performs HTTPS metadata lookup. | Is there a documented receiver-recognized protocol on a named exact target? No receiver protocol falsifies unsolicited custom message delivery. | Cooperative receiver format is publishable as tooling, not a universal popup exploit. |
+
+## Flipper and auxiliary interfaces
+
+Official [Flipper hardware specs](https://docs.flipper.net/zero/development/hardware/tech-specs)
+list BLE 5.4, CC1101 sub-GHz, ST25R3916 NFC, 125 kHz RFID, infrared,
+GPIO and USB. Its [Bad USB guide](https://docs.flipper.net/zero/bad-usb)
+documents USB/BLE HID. [Wi-Fi developer board schematics](https://docs.flipper.net/zero/development/hardware/devboard-schematics)
+show Wi-Fi on an external ESP32-S2 module. These hardware components are not
+created by an M30 app, root shell, chroot or Linux BlueZ port.
+
+## Source and issue-tracker boundaries
+
+The public M30-family repository's current HEAD is `0952065`, advertises GCC
+4.9 and `m30lte_00_defconfig`, and showed zero GitHub Issues at inspection.
+The [TeamWin M30 device tree](https://github.com/TeamWin/android_device_samsung_m30lte)
+links that kernel but does not validate this running recovery. Official
+[Samsung OSRC](https://opensource.samsung.com/) remains the required exact
+source provider. The inspected [REA repo](https://github.com/morluto/rea)
+current HEAD is `1173c0a9dc1b352a60943a491462294db8f353ae`; its presence
+as a tool is not prior evidence of M30 firmware support. No maintained bridge
+or completed SCSC BlueZ port on this exact build was established by this
+search, which is a scoped search result rather than a nonexistence proof.
